ical you  know Papa tell you what what's next on  your list will the big Arizona  immigration law this is the this is  what's kind of that the papers please  law that the police can basically ask  someone if they think they're  undocumented show your you know cities  what is what's the constitutional issue  there that states should not be  enforcing federal immigration law or not  making federal immigration the issue is  is that immigration is traditionally a  federal matter and the federal  government sets these laws in Arizona's  argument is that the federal government  has you know has not even been  holding up its end of the bargain and so  the states then can step in and pick up  the slack and the the federal government  Kabam administration's argument is that  no this is a federal matter the states  can't do this so it's really it's it's a  kind of a federalism states rights quest  what how do you think it'll play out  because you know the the hardcore  supposedly principled conservatives on  the court have gone every which way it  seems in terms of federalist issues  depending on the outcome that they  decide that yeah there's definitely been  every which way there was a case out of  Arizona in the last couple years a  Chamber of Commerce be whiting and this  had to do with an air is a similar air  grown Arizona immigration law but this  required employers to use e-verify to  check the status and there was there  five votes to uphold that so I think  that we're likely to see a repeat of  that I mean the issues are a little  different this is definitely more  controversial but there's some recent  precedent to say that the courts  conservative the five  conservative-leaning you know justices  are going to say that this is a state  matter let the state do it next case  that you're watching the case one case  I've been I've been watching is uncle  saket versus EPA it's out of Idaho this  is basically the Environmental  Protection Agency issues these things  called administrative compliance orders  it's a command to property owners saying  you can't you know stop building this is  a protected wetland and the issue here  is that these administrative compliance  orders these government commands are not  subject to judicial review so this  family the Sacketts they want to go to  court and say the EPA is wrong this is  not a wetland and we want to build and  they understood the law as it stands now  they can't do that so this is actually a  due process case as well as a property  rights one where the issue is do they  get to have their day in court what how  do you think it'll play out I think it's  going to go in their favor the the oral  arguments not just the conservative  judges who you'd expect to be you know  sympathetic but also Justice Breyer  Justice Ginsburg they express basically  the point of view that you know the  entire American jurisprudence you know  has been trending towards more due  process rights rather than less so why  would this regulatory area why would you  know American citizens suddenly have  less due process rights just in this one  area well Damon rude reason senior  editor talking about the latest session  of the Supreme Court of the United  States thanks for talking thank you for  reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie  you
 

